    "narrative": "For Ghost Ride the WHIP on May 30th, our offensive engine is anchored by **Vinnie Pasquantino** (69.49) and **Josh Naylor** (64.20). **Vinnie Pasquantino** benefits from a favorable matchup against Rocker, utilizing a strong platoon advantage to drive his projection toward the upper tier. Simultaneously, **Josh Naylor** capitalizes on a soft matchup against Ryne Nelson, whose xERA of 4.21 allows for significant production in the heart of our order. While SB is our scarcest category, **James Wood** (62.98) remains a must-start; despite a tough opponent in King, his leadoff spot and elite hold on stolen base opportunities make him the most versatile asset in our outfield today. \n\nOur bench management was primarily dictated by health and lineup volatility. **Gabriel Moreno** (46.15) and **Corbin Carroll** (59.57) occupy the \"Lineup Pending\" tier, which forces a cautious approach given the risk of mid-day rest. The most difficult decision involved keeping **Seiya Suzuki** (51.21) in the active lineup despite a 33% rain risk and a tepid matchup, as our secondary options like **Zach McKinstry** or **Dalton Rushing** failed to present a superior efficiency score. We are currently navigating a thin bench due to the IL statuses of **Mickey Moniak**, **Trevor Story**, and **Austin Riley**, which removes any high-ceiling pivots we would typically rotate into the UTIL slot.\n\nLooking ahead, we are monitoring the weather in the **Seiya Suzuki** game closely; should that contest be threatened by the projected precipitation, we are prepared to pivot to the depth of our bench. While **Tyler Soderstrom** provides a +10% park boost, we are tempering expectations against Weathers. We are relying on the combined speed profile of **Corbin Carroll**, **Brice Turang**, and **Travis Bazzana** to maintain our competitive edge in the SB category, even as we face difficult arms like Gray and Woo. We will continue to evaluate the availability of **Giancarlo Stanton**, **Max Muncy**, **Carson Williams**, **Kyle Karros**, **Alex Freeland**, and **Henry Bolte** as roster flexibility remains critical to our efficiency-first strategy.",
